<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 The dark at the side of the stage produced the High Elvis first. He came on walking, in the suit that did none of the shouting, and the Domain rose onto its feet the way a tide comes up a beach \'97 all at once, and without appearing to have decided anything. He crossed to the single microphone at the centre mark and did not reach for it. He stood a moment with his hands folded, a man about to say grace, letting half a million people arrive at the same silence he was already keeping.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 Then there were four figures at the microphone instead of one.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>They had come up no stairs that Sarah had seen. They were simply there, the way weather is there \'97 three string-bean old men with beards to their sternums, each strung about the neck with a ukulele, arranged at the great man\'92s shoulder in a modest backing crescent. She knew them, and the knowing arrived with a small cold drop in the stomach, like a lift starting down. The one in the middle wore a robe the colour of a third-use teabag. The one on his left sweated inside army surplus no army had ever issued, wearing a cat\'92s grin that dripped fang and mischief. The one on his right stood strapped collar to ankle in leather he could not conceivably have chosen for the weather, with the mild expectant readiness of a man waiting to be asked for an autograph. None of the three looked at the crowd. The middle one leaned to the microphone the High Elvis had not yet touched, put his mouth an unhurried inch from it, and, in a whisper the whole tower obligingly amplified to the tree line, said:\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Just a little public service announcement.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah waited for the sky to do something. The sky did nothing. On the perimeter the tanks sat garlanded; overhead the Colonel turned on his tether and took his percentage of the air; and the High Elvis, who had spent the whole afternoon talking stage invasions down off ledges with the patience of a claims assessor, regarded three uninvited prophets and their three small instruments the way a foreman regards a cable run that has turned up stage-left on the schedule after all. He inclined his head. It was not permission, exactly. It was the nod of a man moving an item down the running order to make room for a thing that had, regrettably, always been on it. Then he stepped back half a pace, folded his hands again, and gave them the stage the way you give a colleague the last word \'97 because it costs nothing, and the day is long.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Somewhere in the tower an engineer, confronted with three ukuleles where a headline act ought to be, did the one professional thing available and brought up three faders. The screens, which had captioned every act all day, waited blank and dutiful for something to caption.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Sages played the ditty. They played it once, straight through, no preamble, in the plain strummed unison of men who had rehearsed exactly this and nothing else, a sound with all the menace of a screen door:\
