\f2\i Praise the Dawkins \
\pard\tx360\tx720\tx1080\tx1440\tx1800\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\li1440\fi-360\sl264\slmult1\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 He\'92s the One \
Praise the Dawkins \
And the One after the One \
The Holy Momma \
And her unborn Son \
Praise the Dawkins\
To Kingdom Come\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\i0 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable><!$Scr_Ps::2><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 At her unborn Son, Sarah\'92s hand drifted to her belly before she could stop it. She retrieved it. Beside her the ermine chair held very still.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Then Kleo lifted one hand off the fretboard, the way a conductor asks a room to breathe with him, and set about the business of teaching half a million strangers a song they half-knew already. The refrain was not new to them. Some scrap of it had gone up at the Inauguration and never entirely come down; the crowd had shouted Praise the Dawkins at a screen once and found it fitted the mouth. Now here it was again with a tune attached and an old man to run it, and running it was the entire point \'97 not the words, never the words, but the game.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93The back,\'94 Kleo rasped, and pointed a long finger over the near heads to the far dark provinces of the lawn; and the far dark provinces of the lawn, who could hear him only through the tower\'92s whole cathedral of amplification, sang the refrain back at him, ragged and delighted.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93The front,\'94 he said, and swung the finger down, and the front sang it, louder, competitive on the instant.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Just the Elvi.\'94 And out across the grass the minority who were the genuine article \'97 capes, real sideburns, the pressings themselves \'97 found one another by ear and lifted the line alone, a thinner sound, prouder for being outnumbered.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93The Cloudborne.\'94 And the Cloudborne, who were legion, sang it and buried everybody.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kleo rocked back on his sandals as though a wind had come up off the lawn. He laid a flat hand to his beard, marvelling, unhurried, in the manner of a man noting that the bus had turned up surprisingly full. \'93Well,\'94 he told the microphone. \'93Wasn\'92t that loud.\'94 He let the crowd keep its own laughter a moment. Then he leaned in, confiding, and put the one question no one on that lawn could refuse: \'93Now. Can the Airborne young \'97 the ones who came down from the towers \'97 outdo the Cloudborne?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>And the screens, which had waited blank all this while for something worth the captioning, chose that moment to earn their keep. They cut from black to the platform, and Sarah was looking at herself: thirty feet of her own face thrown up over the lawn, the Holy Momma delivered at last to the cheap seats, blinking mildly down on the multitude that was about to sing her unborn Son at her and had not the first idea whose face it was.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>They could not, and they died trying, and the trying was magnificent: a whole graduating class of astronauts on a beach, bellowing the unborn Son up at a mother whose face hung thirty feet above them and whose actual person sat unremarked on the platform beneath it, and whom not one of them could have named \'97 purely so as not to be beaten at it by the ground. Behind Kleo the other two strummed and did not speak \'97 the one in leather beaming out at the noise as though it were applause meant chiefly for him, the one in surplus grinning his fanged grin at nothing in particular, both of them backing figures, wholly content to be exactly where they were.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>And then Kleo took a solo.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>What followed was, by any technical measure, extraordinary \'97 full of hammers and pulls that would make a Los Angeles Guitar Academy graduate proud, but left this audience wondering why stringed instruments were ever invented in the first place. Half a million people who had, ten seconds earlier, been at open war over a hymn stood and listened to it with the polite, puzzled patience of a congregation that has been asked to admire the vicar\'92s caravan. Not one Air Guitar case discharged. The roar did not so much stop as gently misplace itself, the way conversation dies when someone unexpectedly produces a recorder. Kleo played on into the great baffled quiet, immaculate and unloved, and the tower carried every note of it faithfully to the fig trees.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>On the platform, Aunt Kathy\'92s hand flew to her mouth and stayed there; she came half out of her seat, the tray of medicinal cocktails tilting unregarded on her knee, mouthing something the tower did not amplify and the night did not require. Aunt Hayley did not gasp. Aunt Hayley set her Blue Hawaii down on the bench, with care, the way you put down a thing you have decided you are finished with, and looked at the stage as though noting a licence-plate number. Sarah found her own hand had gone back to her belly a second time, and let it stay, and kept her face precisely where she had left it, which was the most she had ever been able to manage against anything that arrived at exactly the size advertised.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Beside her, Eloise laughed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>She laughed so hard, such as she had not laughed so honestly in decades \'97 a real one, dredged up from under the arithmetic, that shook the ermine on the arms of the chair and left the telemetry tablet forgotten in her lap. She got a hand to her chest and let it run all the way out, unhurried, while below them a ukulele virtuoso finished emptying the tank of a nation, and only when she had the breath back did she say it, to the stage, to the night, to nobody:\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Oh sweet holy spreadsheets, these guys are good for business.\'94\

